# nova.manifest — copy this into the root of every app repo.
#
# This file is the whole convention. `nova` reads nothing else to decide what
# your app is, what it installs, or how it shows up in the app explorer.
#
# ---- identity -------------------------------------------------------------
# NAME         what you type in `nova install <name>`
# DESCRIPTION  ONE line. It is the subtitle in the explorer and what `nova
#              search` matches against, so lead with what the thing does.
# VERSION      your version string; keep it in sync with your git tag.
#              nova installs the latest tag matching ^v?[0-9]+(\.[0-9]+){0,3}$
#              — v1.2.3 counts, v2.0.0-rc1 and nightly do not, so a
#              pre-release never overtakes a release. No version tag at all
#              means your default branch is followed.
# AUTHOR       who maintains it (shown in the detail view)
# HOMEPAGE     project page
# LICENSE      SPDX identifier, e.g. GPL-3.0-or-later
#
# ---- what it is -----------------------------------------------------------
# COMPONENTS   what this app actually installs, space separated. Free-form;
#              list what is really there rather than squeezing it into a
#              category. Recognised for icons and headless handling:
#                 cli  gui  daemon  service  gnome-extension  config
#              Anything containing "gui" is skipped on a machine with no GTK
#              stack, or with --cli-only.
#
# CATEGORY     ONE word, used to group and filter in the explorer and to pick
#              the icon. Use an existing one where it fits:
#                 Security  Network  Media  Development  Office  Files
#                 Desktop   System
#              Anything else still works — it just gets a generic icon.
# TAGS         space separated extra search terms, e.g. "vpn firewall privacy"
#
# ---- privilege ------------------------------------------------------------
# SCOPES       which privilege scopes this app needs, space separated:
#                 user    installs into ~/.local — no root, survives a rebase
#                 system  installs into /usr/local, /etc, system systemd units
#              Declare BOTH if your app has a root service and a desktop part;
#              nova installs each half correctly and the catalog still lists
#              you once. Defaults to `user`.
# ROOT_REASON  required in spirit whenever SCOPES includes `system`: one plain
#              sentence, shown to the user BEFORE nova asks for their password.
#
# ---- presentation (optional, but this is what makes a listing look alive) --
# ICON         repo-relative path to a PNG/SVG, e.g. data/icon.png. Falls back
#              to a category icon from the user's theme.
# SCREENSHOTS  space separated repo-relative image paths, shown in the detail
#              view. They are read from your clone — nothing is fetched from
#              the network.
# ABOUT        repo-relative file shown as the long description
#              (default: README.md)
#
# ---- requirements ---------------------------------------------------------
# DEPENDS      space separated commands your installer needs. nova checks each
#              with `command -v` and shows what is missing before installing.
#
# INSTALLER    installer script relative to repo root (default: install.sh)

NAME=my-tool
DESCRIPTION=Short description of my tool
VERSION=0.1.0
AUTHOR=
HOMEPAGE=
LICENSE=GPL-3.0-or-later

COMPONENTS=cli
CATEGORY=System
TAGS=

SCOPES=user
# ROOT_REASON=installs a systemd service and a config file in /etc

# ICON=data/icon.png
# SCREENSHOTS=data/screenshots/1.png data/screenshots/2.png
# ABOUT=README.md
# DEPENDS=git curl

INSTALLER=install.sh
